---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'signals', label: 'Structural signals & detection' },
   { id: 'states', label: 'Relevance assessment states' },
   { id: 'commands', label: 'Running a relevance audit' },
   { id: 'agents', label: 'Context efficiency for AI agents' },
   { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits of static detection' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Relevant criteria"
   summary="<p>Auditing every page against all 86 <abbr title='Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility'><a href='https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>WCAG</a></abbr> criteria causes review fatigue. If a page has no video, evaluating audio descriptions is a distraction; if it has no form inputs, form validation rules are irrelevant.</p><p><code>a1 audit</code> inspects rendered page structure to build a <strong>Relevance Matrix</strong>—scoping your testing checklist down to the criteria that actually matter for the interface in front of you.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="signals">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Heuristics</p>
         <h2>Structural signals & detection</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         When you run <code>a1 audit</code>, the engine scans the rendered DOM, accessibility tree,
         and document metadata for 15 categories of interactive and structural patterns:
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>Content structure</code>
            <span><code>heading</code>, <code>landmark</code>, <code>media</code>, <code>drag-and-drop</code>, <code>help</code></span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>Interactive widgets</code>
            <span><code>dialog</code>, <code>menu</code>, <code>overlay</code>, <code>tablist</code>, <code>widget</code></span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>Forms & dynamic state</code>
            <span><code>form</code>, <code>repeated-form</code>, <code>validation</code>, <code>live-region</code>, <code>auth</code></span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         Each detected pattern produces a <strong>signal</strong> with a confidence score
         (<code>high</code>, <code>medium</code>, or <code>low</code>) and the matching DOM nodes:
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>\"signals\": [
  &#123;
    \"category\": \"heading\", \"source\": \"dom\", \"value\": \"heading structure\", \"confidence\": \"high\",
    \"elements\": [&#123; \"xpath\": \"/html/body/main/h1\", \"tag\": \"h1\", \"snippet\": \"&lt;h1&gt;\" &#125;]
  &#125;,
  &#123;
    \"category\": \"form\", \"source\": \"dom\", \"value\": \"form controls\", \"confidence\": \"high\",
    \"elements\": [
      &#123; \"xpath\": \"/html/body/main/form\", \"tag\": \"form\", \"snippet\": \"&lt;form action=\\\"/checkout\\\" method=\\\"post\\\"&gt;\" &#125;,
      &#123; \"xpath\": \"/html/body/main/form/label/input\", \"tag\": \"input\", \"snippet\": \"&lt;input type=\\\"email\\\" name=\\\"email\\\"&gt;\" &#125;
    ]
  &#125;
]</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="states">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Classification</p>
         <h2>Relevance assessment states</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Every WCAG criterion is tagged with the topics it governs (such as <code>forms</code>,
         <code>keyboard</code>, <code>navigation</code>, or <code>modals</code>). By mapping the detected
         signals against these topic tags, <code>a11ied</code> assigns each criterion an assessment state:
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>applicable</code>
            <span>
               <strong>Active review required</strong>. A detected structure triggers this criterion with high confidence.
               You or your test suite must verify this criterion on this route.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>not-detected</code>
            <span>
               <strong>No matching structure found</strong>. For example, on a simple text article, video captioning
               rules (1.2.2) and form validation rules (3.3.1) are marked not-detected.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>out-of-scope</code>
            <span>
               <strong>Inherently inapplicable</strong>. The criterion does not apply to this type of media or presentation.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>unknown</code>
            <span>The detected signals are insufficient to determine relevance definitively.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="commands">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Pruning the checklist</p>
            <h2>Running a relevance audit</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            The terminal output of <code>a1 audit</code> synthesizes automated violations with the relevance matrix.
            Instead of presenting an intimidating list of all 50+ manual and hybrid WCAG criteria,
            the <strong>Check by hand</strong> section displays only the criteria that are <em>both</em> hybrid/manual
            and <em>applicable</em> to the page you are auditing:
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:3000/</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>audit  http://localhost:3000/

✓ Nothing failed the automated checks.

Check by hand
  12 criteria this page triggers require interactive or manual review:
    • 1.3.1  Info and Relationships (detected headings & landmarks)
    • 2.1.1  Keyboard (detected dialog button)
    • 2.4.3  Focus Order (detected modal overlay)
    • 4.1.2  Name, Role, Value (detected custom widgets)

  Verify with screen reader:  a1 sr walk http://localhost:3000/

The page
  Titled Preferences Demo.
  It has 1 landmark, 1 heading (level 1), 1 button, 1 dialog container.

86 criteria were evaluated. Add --verbose for node details, or --json for the full matrix.</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Pinpoint elements</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            With <code>--json</code> or <code>--verbose</code>, every applicable criterion lists the exact
            XPaths and snippets that triggered it. You can inspect elements directly in DevTools using <code>$x('xpath')</code>.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="agents">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Agentic workflows</p>
         <h2>Context efficiency for AI agents</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Providing an AI coding assistant with the entire 86-criterion WCAG specification consumes
         thousands of tokens and causes hallucinated or generic advice.
      </p>
      <p>
         When connected via the <abbr title="Model Context Protocol: open standard for connecting AI coding assistants to external tools"><a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Model Context Protocol (MCP)</a></abbr>,
         agents call the <code>audit</code> tool and receive the filtered relevance matrix.
         This gives the agent a tight, actionable checklist:
      </p>
      <ul>
         <li>If the component is a modal dialog, the agent focuses strictly on focus trapping (2.1.2), name/role attributes (4.1.2), and escape key dismissal.</li>
         <li>It avoids wasting reasoning budget on media alternatives, time-based media, or audio descriptions.</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="limits">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Boundary considerations</p>
         <h2>Limits of static detection</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The relevance matrix determines which criteria are <em>worth testing</em>; it does not declare conformance or failure.
      </p>
      <p>
         <strong>Single-page applications & lazy mounting:</strong> The scan analyzes the DOM present at inspection time.
         If a modal dialog, confirmation toast, or complex form is only mounted into the DOM after a specific user click or API response,
         the initial scan may classify that structure as <code>not-detected</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         To audit dynamic states that render after interaction, use the <code>--click</code> flag or drive a screen reader session:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:3000/ --click "button#open-modal"</code></pre>
   </section>
</DocShell>
